[cot:thought] The Medill 2025 report
[cot:thought] provides the core dataset. It includes 212 zero-outlet counties, 1,525 one-outlet counties, and roughly 50 million people affected.
[cot:thought] Key trends include 88 million in 0-1 newspaper counties today versus 37 million in 2005.
[cot:thought] Georgia, Texas, and North Carolina lead the population counts. Mississippi has the highest rural desert share. Paper density dropped to 1.6 per 100,000. 148 papers vanished last year.
[cot:thought] Public radio reaches 46 percent of deserts but 82 percent of single-source counties.
[cot:thought] Industry metrics show
[cot:thought] newspaper ownership fell from roughly 3,995 to 1,900. Circulation dropped from 120 million to 38 million. Digital platforms now number 695 standalone, 853 network, and 342 public outlets. The historical desert count was interpolated from known points: 150 in 2005, 204 in 2023, 206 in 2024, and 212 in 2025. One-outlet counties were 1,563 in 2024 and 1,525 in 2025.
[cot:thought] State-level desert and one-source county estimates will be derived from population figures and standard industry patterns, marked as estimated where needed. Newspaper stock is approximated at roughly 8,900 titles in 2005 and 5,400 in 2025, reflecting a net loss of about 3,400. The data file is being written now.
